JPA Relationships :
In a database, tables are connected to each other which are known as relationships,
Instead of storing everything in one table, databases connect tables using relationships.
JPA helps us create these relationships using annotations.

Types of Relationships :
| Relationship | Meaning                     | Example
| ------------ | --------------------------- |---------------
| OneToOne     | One object ↔ One object     | Teju --> Adharcard
| OneToMany    | One object → Many objects   | IT --> 1. Tej, 2.Teju, 3.Teja, 4.Tejesh
| ManyToOne    | Many objects → One object   | 1. Tej, 2.Teju, 3.Teja, 4.Tejesh --> IT
| ManyToMany   | Many objects ↔ Many objects | Tej --> Java, MySQL, HTML, CSS, Spring, SpringBoot, JDBC
                                             | Java --> 1. Tej, 2.Teju, 3.Teja, 4.Tejesh

@JoinColumn : It tells JPA where to store the foreign key.
Example :
@ManyToOne
@JoinColumn(name="department_id")
private Department department;

Database :
Employee
id
name
department_id

--> Without @JoinColumn, JPA generates a default foreign key name (such as department_id),
but specifying it gives you control over the column name.

Which annotation creates which table :
| Annotation    | Foreign Key Stored In                  |
| ------------- | -------------------------------------- |
| `@OneToOne`   | Owner entity table                     |
| `@OneToMany`  | Child table (when using `@JoinColumn`) |
| `@ManyToOne`  | Many-side table                        |
| `@ManyToMany` | Separate join table                    |
