JPQL (Java Persistence Query Language) & Native Queries :
--> JPQL is an object-oriented query language used to retrieve, update, and delete data from the database
    using entity class names and their fields instead of table names and column names.
--> JPQL is SQL for JPA Entities.

SQL vs JPQL
Suppose we have a table:
Employee Table
| id | name  | salary |
| -- | ----- | ------ |
| 1  | John  | 50000  |
| 2  | David | 60000  |

SQL
SELECT * FROM employee;
Notice:
--> Uses table name
--> Uses column names
JPQL
SELECT e FROM Employee e
Notice:
--> Uses Entity class name
--> Uses Java field names
This is the biggest difference.

Syntax : JPQL is usually written using the @Query annotation.
@Query("JPQL Query")
Example:
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e")
List<Employee> getEmployees();
--> using WHERE 
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.salary > 50000")
List<Employee> getEmployees();
--> Using @Param
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.name = :name")
List<Employee> findEmployee(@Param("name") String name);
--> using Sorting
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e ORDER BY e.salary DESC")
List<Employee> getEmployees();
--> using COUNT
@Query("SELECT COUNT(e) FROM Employee e")
Long countEmployees();
--> using SUM
@Query("SELECT SUM(e.salary) FROM Employee e")
Double totalSalary();
--> using AVG
@Query("SELECT AVG(e.salary) FROM Employee e")
Double averageSalary();
--> using LIKE
Find names starting with "J".
@Query("SELECT e FROM Employee e WHERE e.name LIKE 'J%'")

Native Query : 
A Native Query is a SQL query written using actual database table names and column names instead of entity names.

JPQL vs Native Query :
| JPQL                  | Native Query               |
| --------------------- | -------------------------- |
| Uses Entity names     | Uses Table names           |
| Uses Java field names | Uses Database column names |
| Database-independent  | Database-specific          |
| Recommended           | Use only when needed       |
