Entity Lifecycle :
--> The Entity Lifecycle in JPA describes the different states an entity object goes through from
    its creation until it is removed from the database.
--> An entity doesn't stay in one state forever.
--> It changes its state while interacting with JPA.

Entity Lifecycle States :
--> There are 4 main states
          NEW
           │
      persist()
           │
           ▼
      MANAGED
      /      \
detach()    remove()
   │            │
   ▼            ▼
DETACHED    REMOVED

--> A Transient entity is an object that has been created in Java  
    but is not associated with the Persistence Context and is not stored in the database.
--> A Managed entity is an object that is associated with the Persistence Context and is being tracked by JPA.
--> A Detached entity is an object that was previously managed but is no longer associated with the Persistence Context.
--> A Removed entity is an object that has been marked for deletion and will be deleted from the database 
    when the transaction is committed.
--> Persistence Context is a memory area where JPA stores and manages entity objects during a transaction.
--> Dirty Checking is the process by which JPA automatically detects changes made to Managed entities
    and updates the database when the transaction is committed.
